Creating a simple logging system in PHP involves writing logs to a file in a consistent, manageable way. Below is a basic but extendable logging system.

---

## ✅ Basic PHP Logger Class Example

```php
<?php
class Logger {
    private string $logFile;

    public function __construct(string $filePath = 'app.log') {
        $this->logFile = $filePath;

        // Create log file if it doesn't exist
        if (!file_exists($this->logFile)) {
            file_put_contents($this->logFile, '');
        }
    }

    public function log(string $level, string $message): void {
        $date = date('Y-m-d H:i:s');
        $entry = "[$date] [$level] $message" . PHP_EOL;
        file_put_contents($this->logFile, $entry, FILE_APPEND);
    }

    public function info(string $message): void {
        $this->log('INFO', $message);
    }

    public function warning(string $message): void {
        $this->log('WARNING', $message);
    }

    public function error(string $message): void {
        $this->log('ERROR', $message);
    }
}
```

---

## 🧪 Example Usage

```php
<?php
require_once 'Logger.php';

$logger = new Logger('system.log'); // You can customize the file name

$logger->info("Application started");
$logger->warning("Low disk space");
$logger->error("Database connection failed");
```

